From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian McKenna <brian@brianmckenna.org>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:44:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(shader): cap every loop's iterations per entry with
 APS5_LOOP_GUARD

APS5_LOOP_GUARD=<n> limited the loop exits an invocation evaluated, over all
its loops together. It now limits each loop: the loop counts its back edges in
a variable of its own, cleared on every entry from outside, and its conditional
exits leave once the count reaches n. After that, every other loop of the
invocation leaves at its next conditional exit, so nested loops cannot run for
n^depth iterations, and the invocation records one LoopLimit fault (shader
hash, n, PC of the exit the guard forced) at its return. Loops that finish
sooner run as before. A guest loop whose exit depends on memory the host got
wrong otherwise runs for billions of iterations and hangs the GPU until the
driver resets it.

Programs with loops bind the fault buffer while the guard is on, and the disk
cache keys on the limit. Split-state parts keep their 2^12 default.

Tested: tests/LoopGuardTests.cpp (an endless scalar loop compiles to a module
that validates, compares a per-loop count with the limit at its exit, and
records LoopLimit) passes under Wine, built against Rebox's MinGW build of the
recompiler; as do RequestMemoryView, ShaderMemoryDecoderTolerance,
SpannedUniformity, SsaPassReads, RequestSerializer, SpirvLiteralFloat and
SpirvValidatorExit. With the guard off the emitted SPIR-V is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
